civil rights advocates are asking  federal officials for records about  citizenship checks in Maine the law does  allow such actions near the border but  some are concerned about racial  profiling  here's correspondent Molly line US  Customs and Border Patrol agents in the  far north do far more than keep watch  over the country's frigid border with  Canada  at a bus station in Bangor Maine where  people often hop rides to bigger cities  agents recently asked boarding  passengers if they were US citizens the  American Civil Liberties Union of Maine  finds the practice troubling none of us  want to live in a society where we're  consistently being told we need to show  our papers though they declined our  request for an interview US Customs and  Border Patrol officials confirm they're  agents routinely engaged in operations  at the transportation hubs throughout  the state the law grants immigration  officers broad powers enabling them to  search for aliens on boats planes trains  buses and other vehicles without a  warrant within 100 miles of the border  that puts the whole of Maine within  reach and the entirety or near entirety  of ten other states - ACLU attorneys are  now seeking federal records we don't  know how widespread it is but we're  hoping to learn more it does seem like  there's been an increase in January  Border Patrol agents boarded a bus in  Fort Lauderdale Florida questioning  passengers people whipped out cell  phones and the video went viral one  woman who had overstayed a tourist visa  was removed last week a passenger on an  Amtrak train in Syracuse New York  captured video of agents asking those on  board about their citizenship at bus  stations in Maine reaction was mixed I  am a US citizen I'm proud of it I  appreciate the security as much as  anyone else but I think it's a violation  of our rights and our private  say I would rather be safe than sorry if  an agent approached me I'd be happy to  oblige whatever he asked me US Customs  and Border Protection provided a  statement explaining that enforcement  actions beyond the border are performed  as a means of preventing smuggling and  criminal organizations from exploiting  existing transportation hubs to travel  to the interior of the United States  Shannon our thanks to Molly line
 

